RT64 port patch 0005: vsync pacing (Blast Corps port; MIT, as RT64).

With vsync on, RT64's present thread handed the swap chain a picture every
1 / targetRate s on its own timer, and plume's D3D12 swap chain presents with
a sync interval of 0 (present wait): the display shows whichever picture is
newest at each refresh.  At a target rate equal to the display's the timer
beat against the refreshes: some refreshes got two pictures (one never shown)
and the next none (measured with DXGI frame statistics on a 60 Hz display:
18 % of the shown intervals 33 ms instead of 16.7 in a run at 60).

With this patch (PresentQueue::holdFor, waitForHold, swapChainPresent):
every picture is presented with a sync interval of 1 (D3D12; Vulkan's FIFO
swap chain is the same), so none is dropped, and the timer is skipped.  The
picture before stays
  - for in-between (interpolated) pictures: 1 refresh at the display's rate,
    N at 1/N of it (other target rates: RT64's timer, as before);
  - for a game picture: the refreshes between its submission
    (Present::submitTime, set in State::advancePresent) and the next one's,
    less those of its in-between pictures; a hold over one refresh waits for
    the middle of the refresh before the one due, counted from when the
    picture before was shown (DXGI frame statistics; Vulkan: the end of the
    frame-latency wait), so the phase of the game's retraces against the
    display's doesn't matter.
Nothing is held while another present waits.  The host can turn it off
(PresentQueue::vsyncPacing) and watch every present (presentedHook).  RT64's
interpolation weights, the workload queue and the game's timing are
untouched.

